  "tipWarning": "Warning",
  "tipBaseboardSealantTitle": "Finish skirting boards with sealant",
  "tipBaseboardSealantSteps": "Dust the top of the skirting and the wall, they must be dry.\nBetween skirting and wall, use a paintable acrylic sealant; at the bottom, against the floor, a flexible sealant (silicone or polyurethane) that follows the movement of the wood floor.\nPut masking tape on either side of the joint if you want a clean line.\nCut the nozzle at an angle, just a little narrower than the gap to fill.\nRun the bead in one steady movement, pushing the gun rather than pulling it.\nSmooth with a finger dipped in soapy water, or with a smoothing tool.\nRemove the tape straight away, before the sealant skins over.\nWait for the drying time shown on the cartridge before painting the acrylic.",
  "tipBaseboardGlueTitle": "Glue a skirting board",
  "tipBaseboardGlueSteps": "The wall must be clean, dry and dust-free; sand or scrape off old flaking paint.\nCut and dry-fit all the pieces before gluing, corners included.\nApply the grab adhesive on the back in a wavy bead or in dabs every 20 to 30 cm, 1 cm from the edges.\nSit the skirting on a thin packer to keep the gap with the floating floor, then press hard against the wall.\nHold it while it sets with masking tape or clamps on the corners.\nWipe off any adhesive that squeezes out straight away, with a damp cloth.",
  "tipBaseboardNailsTitle": "Nail a skirting board",
  "tipBaseboardNailsSteps": "Find the tracks or studs behind a plasterboard partition: that's where the nail holds.\nPre-drill the ends of each piece so the wood doesn't split.\nDrive lost-head nails or panel pins every 40 cm, and one at each end.\nSink the head below the surface with a nail punch, without hitting the skirting with the hammer.\nFill the holes with wood filler, sand, then paint or varnish.",
  "tipBaseboardNailsWarning": "Avoid nailing above or below a socket or switch: cables often run vertically there. A cable detector keeps you from drilling into them.",
  "tipCrookedWallsTitle": "Walls that aren't straight",
  "tipCrookedWallsSteps": "Measure the real angle of each corner with “Measure the angle” in the wall entry: two marks and the gap between them are enough.\nCut a little long first, dry-fit, then take off small passes with the saw until the corner closes.\nIn a very open or very closed inside corner, a scribed cut (the profile of one skirting cut into the other) closes better than a mitre.\nAgainst a bowed wall, glue rather than nails: the skirting follows the wall while it is pressed during setting.\nA gap under 3 mm is filled with acrylic sealant; wider, the cut needs redoing.",
  "tipParquetMarginTitle": "Always plan a little more",
  "tipParquetMarginSteps": "A 5% margin is enough for a rectangular room laid straight.\nAllow 10% for an awkward room, lots of obstacles or diagonal laying.\nAllow 15% for herringbone or chevron.\nBuy all the packs at the same time, with the same batch number: the shade varies from one production run to the next.\nKeep a whole pack after laying, to replace a damaged board later.",
  "tipDoorSealTitle": "Fit a door seal",
  "tipDoorSealSteps": "Close the door on a sheet of paper: where it slides out without resistance, air gets through.\nMeasure the gap between the door and its frame to choose the seal thickness.\nClean the frame rebate with methylated spirit and let it dry.\nStick the adhesive seal on the frame, against the stop, without stretching it.\nMitre it at the top corners so it stays continuous.\nAt the bottom of the door, screw on a brush or lip door sweep, set to just brush the floor.\nRedo the paper test: it should be gripped all the way round.",
  "tipAirConMaintenanceTitle": "Maintain an air conditioner",
  "tipAirConMaintenanceSteps": "Switch the unit off with the remote, then at the breaker, before any work.\nTake the filters out of the indoor unit, rinse them in lukewarm water without detergent, let them dry in the shade.\nClean the fins with a soft brush, without bending them.\nCheck the condensate drain: pour a little water into the tray, it should drain away without overflowing; a blocked pipe is cleared with vinegar water.\nClear leaves and anything blocking the air within 50 cm around the outdoor unit.\nIf the unit no longer cools, frosts up or leaks on the gas side, call a professional.",
  "tipAirConMaintenanceWarning": "The refrigerant circuit is not something to open yourself: French law reserves it for a company holding a capacity certificate. Have the leak test done by a professional, according to your unit's charge.",
