  "termsTitle": "Trade words (tap for the explanation)",
  "glossaryTitle": "Glossary",
  "termRafter": "Rafter",
  "termRafterText": "Timber that slopes down from the top of the roof to the wall, every 50 to 60 cm. The battens and tiles rest on the rafters, and the insulation goes between them.",
  "termRidge": "Ridge",
  "termRidgeText": "The top of the roof, where the two slopes meet. By extension, the beam running along the very top.",
  "termSlope": "Slope",
  "termSlopeText": "The sloping ceiling under the roof, in the loft. It's usually lined with plasterboard screwed onto tracks.",
  "termKneeWall": "Knee wall",
  "termKneeWallText": "The short low wall, often 60 cm to 1 m, at the foot of the slope in the loft. Behind it there's often a lost space that can be used for storage.",
  "termHeader": "Trimmer",
  "termHeaderText": "Timber laid across to pick up a rafter or joist that has been cut, for example to fit a roof window or stairs. The two neighbouring pieces are doubled to carry the load.",
  "termJoist": "Joist",
  "termJoistText": "A floor beam, laid at regular intervals. The panel floor is screwed on top.",
  "termSpacing": "Spacing",
  "termSpacingText": "The distance from the centre of one piece to the centre of the next (rafters, joists, studs). Easier to measure than the space between them.",
  "termPlasterboard": "Plasterboard (12.5 mm)",
  "termPlasterboardText": "Plasterboard 12.5 mm thick, screwed onto tracks or furring channels to make walls and ceilings.",
  "termCarrez": "Carrez area",
  "termCarrezText": "The living area as defined by French law: only where the ceiling is 1.80 m or higher. In a loft, it's much smaller than the floor area.",
  "termReveal": "Reveal",
  "termRevealText": "The “tunnel” between the window and the room, as deep as the wall or roof. Its sides are lined with boards or planks.",
  "termFrame": "Frame",
  "termFrameText": "The fixed frame of a window, fixed into the wall or roof. A window that opens also has a “sash”; a fixed window only has the frame.",
  "termStile": "Stile",
  "termStileText": "Vertical piece of a frame. In a window, the stiles run from top to bottom.",
  "termRail": "Rail",
  "termRailText": "Horizontal piece of a frame, set between the stiles.",
  "termTenon": "Tenon",
  "termTenonText": "The end of a piece of wood cut thinner, which fits into a hole in the other piece. Glued, it makes a very strong joint without screws.",
  "termMortise": "Mortise",
  "termMortiseText": "The rectangular hole cut to take the tenon, at the same width and depth.",
  "termRebate": "Rebate",
  "termRebateText": "The step-shaped recess along the frame, where the glass sits.",
  "termGlazingBead": "Glazing bead",
  "termGlazingBeadText": "The small strip nailed or screwed on to hold the glass in the rebate. You take it off to change the glass.",
  "termSetting": "Setting block",
  "termSettingText": "Small hard plastic block set under the glass so it doesn't touch the wood and its weight rests in the right place.",
  "liningTitle": "Roof window reveal",
  "liningHelp": "The tunnel between the roof window and the room, cut to let in the most light.",
  "liningOpening": "Opening of {length} cm in the slope",
  "liningGain": "{gain} cm more than a straight reveal: more light at the back of the room",
  "liningTop": "Top of the reveal flat, horizontal: {length} cm deep",
  "liningBottom": "Bottom of the reveal plumb, vertical: {length} cm high",
  "liningBoard": "{area} m² of board for the four sides, offcuts not counted",
  "liningWhyTop": "The top runs horizontally from the window to the ceiling: thickness ({depth} cm) divided by the sine of the slope ({slope}°).",
  "liningWhyBottom": "The bottom drops straight down to the ceiling: thickness divided by the cosine of the slope ({slope}°).",
  "liningWhyOpening": "Opening in the ceiling: window height ({height} cm) plus {gain} cm gained at the top and bottom.",
  "liningSteps": "Order: fit the window and its flashing, cut rafters and insulation to the opening above, trimmers at top and bottom, vapour barrier connected to the window frame with the right tape, then the four sides in board and the corners with reinforced tape.",
  "liningWindowWidth": "Window width (cm)",
  "liningWindowHeight": "Window height (cm)",
  "liningDepth": "Slope thickness: rafter, insulation, board (cm)",
  "roofWindowReference": "Reference of the existing window, on its plate (e.g. MK08 or 308)",
  "roofWindowReferenceSize": "Frame of {width} × {height} cm: the new window with the same reference takes its place",
  "roofWindowReferenceUnknown": "Reference not recognised: it's written as one letter, sometimes a second, and two digits (CK04, MK08), or three digits on older windows (308). The plate is at the top of the frame, with the window open.",
  "termWhatTitle": "What it is",
  "termUseTitle": "What it's for",
  "termRafterUse": "Carry the roof: tiles, snow and wind rest on the rafters. They're never cut without picking them up with a trimmer.",
  "termRidgeUse": "Hold the tops of the rafters and close the top of the roof. It's the highest point of the room in the loft.",
  "termSlopeUse": "Close the room under the roof and hold the insulation. It's what gets lined with plasterboard.",
  "termKneeWallUse": "Close the bottom of the loft, where you can't stand, and hide the insulation and ducts. Its height changes the living area.",
  "termHeaderUse": "Open the roof or a floor without weakening it: it transfers the load of the cut piece onto the neighbouring ones.",
  "termJoistUse": "Carry the floor and what's put on it. Their section and spacing depend on the span.",
  "termSpacingUse": "Know where to screw and how many pieces to plan: you measure from centre to centre, always the same way.",
  "termPlasterboardUse": "Make smooth walls and ceilings, ready to paint, and hide the insulation. Faster and lighter than plaster.",
  "termCarrezUse": "Know the really usable area of a room in the loft, and the one that counts for a sale.",
  "termRevealUse": "Let the light from the window into the room. Well cut, it lights much more.",
  "termFrameUse": "Hold the glass and fix into the opening. It's the part that stays in place for good.",
  "termStileUse": "Form the sides of the frame. They take the mortises.",
  "termRailUse": "Form the top and bottom of the frame. Their tenons go into the stiles.",
  "termTenonUse": "Join two pieces of wood firmly, without screws or brackets: glued, a tenon lasts for decades.",
  "termMortiseUse": "Take the tenon. The tighter it fits, the stronger the joint.",
  "termRebateUse": "House the glass: it sits in the step, protected from rain, and the glazing bead holds it on the other side.",
  "termGlazingBeadUse": "Hold the glass in place without glue, and be able to change it one day by taking it off.",
  "termSettingUse": "Stop the glass touching the wood and breaking, and spread its weight over the frame.",
