  "oopsTitle": "I made a mistake",
  "oopsHelp": "Loose tenon, warped board, misplaced hole, run, failed sealant: the cause, the repair and what you keep.",
  "oopsIntro": "Almost everything can be put right. Before throwing a piece away, see whether it can be repaired or recut smaller.",
  "oopsLooseTenon": "Tenon too loose",
  "oopsWarpedBoard": "Warped board",
  "oopsMisplacedHole": "Misplaced hole",
  "oopsVarnishRun": "Varnish run",
  "oopsFailedSealant": "Failed sealant joint",
  "oopsShortCut": "Piece cut too short",
  "oopsLooseTenonCause": "Tenon cheeks planed too thin, or mortise cut wider than the marking.",
  "oopsWarpedBoardCause": "Wood not dry enough, stored on end or in the sun, or finished on one face only.",
  "oopsMisplacedHoleCause": "Measurement taken from the wrong edge, or the bit's point slipped at the start.",
  "oopsVarnishRunCause": "Coat too thick, especially on a vertical surface.",
  "oopsFailedSealantCause": "Uneven bead, smoothed too late, or dusty or damp surface.",
  "oopsShortCutCause": "Measurement read beside the mark, or saw cut on the wrong side.",
  "oopsLooseTenon1": "Glue a thin veneer or a slip of wood onto one cheek of the tenon, grain the same way.",
  "oopsLooseTenon2": "Once dry, take it back to thickness with a plane or chisel until it goes in with hand pressure.",
  "oopsLooseTenon3": "For a small gap, a gap-filling glue (epoxy) is enough; check the instructions.",
  "oopsWarpedBoard1": "A cupped board: dampen the hollow side, lay it flat under weights for a day or two.",
  "oopsWarpedBoard2": "If it stays warped, rip it into narrower strips, which straighten better.",
  "oopsWarpedBoard3": "Then store the wood flat, on battens, under cover.",
  "oopsMisplacedHole1": "Drill the hole to the diameter of a wooden dowel, glue in the dowel, grain the same way as the piece.",
  "oopsMisplacedHole2": "Trim it flush once the glue is dry, then sand.",
  "oopsMisplacedHole3": "Drill the right hole at least one and a half diameters away from the plugged one, or use a dab of wood filler for a small hidden hole.",
  "oopsVarnishRun1": "Don't touch anything while it's wet: wait for the full drying time given on the tin.",
  "oopsVarnishRun2": "Shave off the run with a steel rule or a knife held flat, then sand with fine grit (240).",
  "oopsVarnishRun3": "Dust off and apply a thin coat, crossing then smoothing with the grain.",
  "oopsFailedSealant1": "Cut out the joint with a knife and clean: dry surface, no dust or silicone residue.",
  "oopsFailedSealant2": "Put masking tape on each side of the joint.",
  "oopsFailedSealant3": "Run an even bead again, smooth straight away with a spatula, and remove the tape before it sets.",
  "oopsShortCut1": "Measure again: a few millimetres can sometimes be made up with a joint or a skirting.",
  "oopsShortCut2": "Otherwise, keep the piece for a shorter cut on the same project.",
  "oopsShortCut3": "Cut a new piece, marking the waste side with a cross before sawing.",
  "oopsLooseTenonReuse": "Nothing to throw away: the joint stays strong once fixed.",
  "oopsWarpedBoardReuse": "Cut short or ripped, it makes battens, packers or short pieces.",
  "oopsMisplacedHoleReuse": "The piece stays in place; a well-aligned dowel barely shows under the finish.",
  "oopsVarnishRunReuse": "The piece stays as it is, the new coat hides the repair.",
  "oopsFailedSealantReuse": "The opened cartridge can be kept: plug the nozzle with a nail or tape.",
  "oopsShortCutReuse": "A long offcut goes with the offcuts for a future project.",
